/* Imported from 2026 JUNE.docx, Q.51-Q.140. Answer keys are independently reviewed where marked; ambiguous/flawed items are flagged. */
const JUNE_2026_QUESTIONS = [{"id":"J26-51","question":"Match the LIST-I with LIST-II LIST-I (Articles / Books) A. General and Industrial Management B. Papers on the Science of Administration C. The Science of Administration: Three Problems D. Reflections on Public Administration LIST-II (Authors) I. Robert Dahl II. Henri Fayol III. John M. Gaus IV. Gullick and Urwick","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"answer":1,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":51,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The titles match as follows: General and Industrial Management—Henri Fayol; Papers on the Science of Administration—Gulick and Urwick; The Science of Public Administration: Three Problems—Robert Dahl; Reflections on Public Administration—John M. Gaus.","options":["Correct: A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III.","Incorrect: assigns General and Industrial Management to Dahl and other titles to wrong authors.","Incorrect: Fayol did not author Papers on the Science of Administration as an edited volume; Dahl did not author Reflections.","Incorrect: the author-title pairs are mismatched."],"sources":[{"label":"Smithsonian Libraries — Papers on the Science of Administration (1937)","url":"https://www.si.edu/object/papers-science-administration-luther-gulick-l-urwick-james-d-mooney-and-others-edited-luther-gulick%3Asiris_sil_45201"},{"label":"Fayol — General and Industrial Management","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Henri+Fayol+General+and+Industrial+Management"},{"label":"Robert Dahl — The Science of Public Administration: Three Problems","url":"https://www.google.com/search?q=Robert+Dahl+The+Science+of+Public+Administration+Three+Problems"}]}},{"id":"J26-52","question":"Arrange the set up of following commissions in chronological order:- A. National Commission for Backward Classes. B. National Commission for Safai Karmacharis. C. National Commission for Minorities. D. National Commission for Women. E. National Human Rights Commission.","options":["A, B, C, D, E","C, D, E, B, A","D, C, A, E, B","E, C, D, B, A"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":52,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The official establishment dates give this order: National Commission for Women (31 January 1992), National Commission for Minorities (17 May 1992), National Commission for Backward Classes (14 August 1993), National Human Rights Commission (12 October 1993), and National Commission for Safai Karamcharis (12 August 1994). The corrected third option is the only matching sequence.","options":["Incorrect: places the 1993 and 1994 commissions before the 1992 commissions.","Incorrect: puts the National Commission for Safai Karamcharis before the NCBC, although it was constituted in 1994.","Correct: D, C, A, E, B follows the official establishment dates.","Incorrect: puts NHRC before the two 1992 commissions."],"sources":[{"label":"National Commission for Women — Brief History","url":"https://uatncwwomen.digitalindiacorporation.in/about-us/about-the-commission/brief-history/"},{"label":"Government of India Gazette — Commission establishment dates","url":"https://nih.ayush.gov.in/assets/uploads/pdf/content_en_pdf1745919134.pdf"},{"label":"NHRC — About the Organisation","url":"https://nhrc.nic.in/about-the-organisation"},{"label":"National Commission for Safai Karamcharis — About NCSK","url":"https://ncsk.nic.in/api-web-base-about-ncsk"}]}},{"id":"J26-53","question":"Which of the following is correct about Cabinet Secretariat:-","options":["It is under the direct charge of President of India.","In the Government of India (allocation of business) rules 1961 which finds a place in the third schedule to the rules.","In 1946, Executive Council Secretariat designated as Cabinet Secretariat.","The administrative head of Cabinet Secretariat is Chief Secretary."],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":53,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The Cabinet Secretariat's official history states that on 5 September 1946 the Executive Council Secretariat was designated as the Cabinet Secretariat. Its official description also says it functions directly under the Prime Minister and is headed administratively by the Cabinet Secretary.","options":["Incorrect — it functions under the Prime Minister, not directly under the President.","Incorrect — the Allocation of Business Rules assign functions to the Cabinet Secretariat, but this wording does not accurately identify the correct historical statement.","Correct — the official Cabinet Secretariat history confirms the 5 September 1946 designation.","Incorrect — the administrative head is the Cabinet Secretary, not the Chief Secretary."],"sources":[{"label":"Cabinet Secretariat — Origin","url":"https://cabsec.gov.in/aboutus/origin"},{"label":"Cabinet Secretariat — official functions","url":"https://cabsec.nic.in/index.php"}]}},{"id":"J26-54","question":"Consider the following statements regarding the appointment of the Chief Secretary. A. The Chief Secretary is chosen by the Chief Minister B. The senior most officer of the state is appointed as the Chief Secretary C. The Chief Secretary is always appointed on the basis of performance and merit. D. There is no fixed tenure of the Chief Secretary","options":["B & C Only","D & C Only","A & D Only","C & A Only"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":54,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The Chief Secretary is chosen by the Chief Minister, and the post has no fixed tenure. The claim that the senior-most officer must invariably be appointed is too absolute; selection is not governed by an automatic seniority rule. The claim that appointment is always based on performance and merit is also not a binding universal rule. Therefore A and D only.","options":["Incorrect: seniority alone does not create an automatic rule that the senior-most officer must be appointed.","Incorrect: there is no fixed tenure, but statement C is not a universal appointment rule.","Correct: the Chief Minister chooses the Chief Secretary, and there is no fixed tenure.","Incorrect: statement C is too absolute; A is correct, but C is not."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact Chief Secretary question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/consider-the-following-statements-regarding-the-ap--6a84149077472744b61cd505"},{"label":"Drishti IAS — Chief Secretary appointment and tenure","url":"https://www.drishtiias.com/state-pcs-current-affairs/haryanas-new-chief-secretary-1"}]}},{"id":"J26-55","question":"\"Hypotheses are the problems behind the hypotheses.\" Who said this?","options":["Ranjit Kumar","C.R. Kothari","Fred N. Kerlinger","William J. Goods and Paul K. Hatt."],"answer":null,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":55,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Flagged — conflicting answer attribution","basis":"The question-specific Testbook page is internally contradictory: its answer heading labels option 3 (Fred N. Kerlinger), while its detailed explanation explicitly says the quotation is by William J. Goode and Paul K. Hatt (option 4). The wording also appears in a poorly reproduced form. Because the source itself gives incompatible answers and no stronger primary-text confirmation was located, this item is flagged rather than scored.","options":["Not confirmed: Ranjit Kumar is not supported by the located question-specific source.","Not confirmed: C. R. Kothari is not supported by the located question-specific source.","Conflicting source label: the page's answer heading names Kerlinger, but its explanation contradicts that label.","Conflicting source explanation: the page's detailed text names Goode and Hatt, but its answer heading labels option 3."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact question; answer heading conflicts with detailed explanation","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/hypotheses-are-the-problems-behind-the-hypotheses--6a84149321fd595ca6de9d0e"}]}},{"id":"J26-56","question":"Which of the following statement is not correct regarding Disaster Management in India.?","options":["Ministry of Home Affairs is nodal ministry for disaster management.","National Disaster Management Authority was set up under the chairmanship of Home Minister.","Under Disaster Management Act 2005, there is provision of National Disaster Mitigation Fund.","National Institute of Disaster Management was setup for capacity building purpose."],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":56,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The NDMA is chaired by the Prime Minister under section 3 of the Disaster Management Act, 2005. Section 47 provides for the National Disaster Mitigation Fund. The MHA is the nodal ministry and NIDM has a capacity-building role.","options":["Correct — MHA is the nodal ministry for disaster management.","Incorrect — NDMA is chaired by the Prime Minister, not the Home Minister.","Correct — section 47 provides for the National Disaster Mitigation Fund.","Correct — NIDM's role includes training, research and capacity building."],"sources":[{"label":"Disaster Management Act, section 3","url":"https://indiacode.nic.in/"},{"label":"Disaster Management Act, section 47","url":"https://indiacode.nic.in/"}]}},{"id":"J26-57","question":"Match the LIST-I with LIST-II LIST-I A. Top-down Approach B. Bottom-up Approach C. Policy-Action Approach D. Managerial Approach LIST-II I. Re-inventing government perspective more business like II. See policy makers as the central actors III. Implementation has perspective of street-level bureaucrats IV. Behavioral approach view the process of implementations policy action continuum","options":["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":57,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Top-down analysis treats policy-makers as central actors; bottom-up analysis emphasizes street-level implementers; the policy-action approach examines the policy/implementation continuum; the managerial approach draws on business-like reinvention.","options":["Incorrect: top-down is II, not I; bottom-up is III, not II.","Incorrect: repeats I and assigns the approaches incorrectly.","Incorrect: top-down is II, and the other approaches are mismatched.","Correct: A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I."],"sources":[{"label":"IGNOU eGyanKosh — Public Policy: Implementation System and Models","url":"https://egyankosh.ac.in/bitstream/123456789/25776/1/Unit-12.pdf"}]}},{"id":"J26-58","question":"Which of the following is Not correct regarding Central Social Welfare Board.?","options":["It was set up in August 1953","Dr. Durgabai Deshmukh was founder chairperson of the Board.","Board developed family counselling concept.","Board did not promote voluntary efforts in social welfare."],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":58,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The Central Social Welfare Board was established in 1953 under the leadership of Durgabai Deshmukh and worked to support and strengthen voluntary organisations in social welfare. The statement that it did not promote voluntary efforts is therefore the incorrect statement.","options":["Incorrect: the Board was established in 1953.","Incorrect: Durgabai Deshmukh was its founding Chairperson.","Incorrect: the Board promoted welfare services including family counselling; this is not the false statement in the item.","Correct: this is false because promoting voluntary effort and voluntary organisations was central to the Board's role."],"sources":[{"label":"Maharashtra Government — Central Social Welfare Board","url":"https://womenchild.maharashtra.gov.in/en/central-social-welfare-board"},{"label":"Central Social Welfare Board — official website","url":"https://cswb.gov.in/"}]}},{"id":"J26-59","question":"Who of the following thinkers define \"Public Administration, is a field and practice that has its origin in the earliest times of human civilisation.\"?","options":["Luther Gulick","Lyndall Urwick","Dwight Waldo","Woodrow Wilson"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":59,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Dwight Waldo is the intended answer. The same question appears in the June 2026 UGC NET Public Administration paper with Waldo identified as the answer. The statement reflects the historical breadth of administration, which predates the modern academic discipline.","options":["Incorrect: Luther Gulick is associated with POSDCORB and classical administrative theory, not this definition.","Incorrect: Lyndall Urwick is associated with classical organisation theory.","Correct: Dwight Waldo is identified with this definition in the question-specific reference.","Incorrect: Woodrow Wilson is associated with the modern study of public administration, especially his 1887 essay."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — UGC NET June 2026 question and solution","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/who-of-the-following-thinkers-define-public-admin--6a84148d1f0d7a4b63d21aa6/amp"}]}},{"id":"J26-60","question":"Which of the followings is correct regarding incrementalism model of policy making. A. It is conservatives model. B. It is most politically feasible model. C. It is goal-focused model. D. It is descriptive model. E. It assumes that policy makers lack the brains, time and money to fashion systemic and innovative policies.","options":["A, B, C, D Only","A, B, D, E Only","B, C, D, E Only","A, B, C, D, E"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":60,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Lindblom's incrementalism is a descriptive, politically feasible approach based on successive limited comparisons and constrained decision-makers. It is commonly characterized as conservative; it is not a goal-focused comprehensive-rational model. Thus A, B, D and E are correct.","options":["Correct: incrementalism tends to preserve existing arrangements and make marginal changes, hence the conservative label.","Correct: limited comparisons and partisan mutual adjustment make it politically feasible.","Incorrect: the model is not a goal-focused comprehensive-rational approach; it emphasizes limited, incremental adjustment.","Correct: it describes how policy decisions are often actually made under limits of time, information and resources."],"sources":[{"label":"Lindblom, The Science of Muddling Through (1959)","url":"https://gsdm.u-tokyo.ac.jp/file/Lindblom-1959.pdf"},{"label":"Oxford Handbook — The Science of Muddling Through","url":"https://academic.oup.com/edited-volume/27993/chapter-abstract/211702556"}]}},{"id":"J26-61","question":"Which commission / committee recommended for the setting up of 'Sector Corporations' in India?","options":["Krishna Menon Committee","Kothari Committee","First Administrative Reforms Commission","Second Administrative Reforms Commission"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":61,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The First Administrative Reforms Commission recommended the creation of Sector Corporations in its report on Public Sector Undertakings (October 1967). The Government did not accept the proposal, but the recommendation is attributed to the First ARC.","options":["Incorrect: not the committee identified in the relevant public-sector reform recommendation.","Incorrect: the Kothari Committee is not credited with this recommendation.","Correct: the First ARC recommended Sector Corporations in its 1967 report on public sector undertakings.","Incorrect: the recommendation predates the Second ARC."],"sources":[{"label":"Third Pay Commission report citing the First ARC recommendation","url":"https://www.cgspublicationindia.com/Pdf/Pay%20Commissions/Third%20Pay%20Commission/3rd%20CPC%20%28Report%2C%20Vol%20I%29.pdf"}]}},{"id":"J26-62","question":"Match the LIST-I with LIST-II LIST-I A. Village Panchayat B. Panchayat Samiti C. Municipal Council D. Municipal Corporation LIST-II I. President II. Mayor III. Block Development Officer IV. Sarpanch","options":["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":62,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The intended matches are Village Panchayat—Sarpanch (IV), Panchayat Samiti—Block Development Officer (III), Municipal Council—President (I), and Municipal Corporation—Mayor (II). This is option 4.","options":["Incorrect: the village panchayat and municipal corporation are not matched with their usual heads.","Incorrect: assigns the village panchayat to Mayor and municipal corporation to President.","Incorrect: assigns the village panchayat to BDO and Panchayat Samiti to Sarpanch.","Correct: A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II."],"sources":[{"label":"Ministry of Panchayati Raj — Panchayati Raj institutions","url":"https://panchayat.gov.in/"},{"label":"Ministry of Housing and Urban Affairs — Urban local bodies","url":"https://mohua.gov.in/"}]}},{"id":"J26-63","question":"Match the LIST-I with LIST-II LIST-I A. Cluster Sampling B. Sample C. Sample Design D. Sampling Frame LIST-II I. The names of all items of a universe II. A technique or the procedure adopted by a researcher to select items for sample III. The number of items to be selected from the universe IV. Dividing the total population into a number of relatively small sub-divisions or units","options":["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":63,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Cluster sampling divides a population into smaller groups or clusters (IV); a sample is the selected subset/number of units (III); sample design is the procedure for selecting the sample (II); and a sampling frame is the list of population units (I). The correct sequence is A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I, option 3.","options":["Incorrect: cluster sampling is not the list of all population units.","Incorrect: sample design and cluster sampling are mismatched.","Correct: A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I.","Incorrect: the sample and cluster sampling definitions are reversed."],"sources":[{"label":"OpenStax — Sampling and data collection","url":"https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics/pages/1-1-definitions-of-statistics-probability-and-key-terms"},{"label":"FAO — Sampling methods and sampling frames","url":"https://www.fao.org/4/x5684e/x5684e04.htm"}]}},{"id":"J26-64","question":"Which of the following is correct about Normative-Optimal Model? A. Balancing the Rational and Extra-Rational factors. B. It is quantitative, not qualitative. C. It deals with meta-policy making. D. Its basic rational is to be economically rational.","options":["A, B, C, D Only","A, C, D Only","B, C, D Only","A, B, D Only"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":64,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Yehezkel Dror’s normative-optimal model combines rational and extra-rational elements and includes meta-policy making. It is qualitative rather than merely quantitative, so A, C and D are correct.","options":["Correct: the model balances rational analysis with extra-rational elements such as experience, intuition and value judgement.","Incorrect: standard descriptions characterize the model as qualitative, not as quantitative only.","Correct: meta-policy making is one of the model’s three broad stages.","Correct: it retains economic rationality while supplementing it with extra-rational considerations."],"sources":[{"label":"Alagappa University — Administrative Thinkers (Dror’s Normative-Optimal Model)","url":"https://mis.alagappauniversity.ac.in/siteAdmin/dde-admin/uploads/3/__UG_B.A._Public%20Administration%20%28English%29_106%2034%20Administrative%20Thinkers_9840.pdf"},{"label":"Testbook — Normative-Optimal Model question and solution","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/which-of-the-following-is-correct-about-normative--6a8414a42301e182be9e556b"}]}},{"id":"J26-65","question":"Arrange the activities conducted by Union Public Service Commission for the recruitment of Civil Services in India. A. Advertisement of vacant posts. B. Scrutiny of the applications. C. Conducting Preliminary Extermination. D. Certification E. Conducting Mains Examination and Interview","options":["A, B, C, D, E","A, B, C, E, D","D, E, B, C, A","C, B, A, D, E"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":65,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"UPSC's Civil Services Examination has Preliminary followed by Main (written and interview). The commission's process starts with notification/advertisement and applications; certification/selection documentation follows the examination stages. The intended sequence is A, B, C, E, D.","options":["Incorrect: places certification before the Main examination and interview.","Correct: advertisement, scrutiny, Preliminary, Main/interview, then certification.","Incorrect: starts with certification and puts advertisement last.","Incorrect: starts with Preliminary before advertisement and scrutiny."],"sources":[{"label":"UPSC — Examination structure","url":"https://cms.upsc.gov.in/about-us/divisions/examination/"}]}},{"id":"J26-66","question":"Arrange the following books in chronological order, according to their year of first publication: A. The Ecology of Public Administration B. The Human Side of Enterprise C. Administrative Behaviour D. The Principles of Scientific Management E. The Functions of the Executive","options":["A, B, D, E, C","B, D, C, A, E","C, D, A, E, B","D, E, C, B, A"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":66,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"First publication years: Principles of Scientific Management (1911), Functions of the Executive (1938), Administrative Behavior (1947), The Human Side of Enterprise (1960), and The Ecology of Public Administration (1961). The order is D, E, C, B, A.","options":["Incorrect: puts The Ecology of Public Administration first.","Incorrect: puts The Human Side of Enterprise before the 1911 book.","Incorrect: starts Administrative Behavior before Scientific Management.","Correct: D, E, C, B, A is the chronological sequence."],"sources":[{"label":"Taylor — Principles of Scientific Management","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Taylor+Principles+of+Scientific+Management+1911"},{"label":"Barnard — Functions of the Executive","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Chester+Barnard+Functions+of+the+Executive+1938"},{"label":"Simon — Administrative Behavior","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Herbert+Simon+Administrative+Behavior+1947"},{"label":"McGregor — Human Side of Enterprise","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Douglas+McGregor+Human+Side+of+Enterprise+1960"},{"label":"Gaus — Ecology of Public Administration","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=John+Gaus+Ecology+of+Public+Administration"}]}},{"id":"J26-67","question":"Which of the following statements about National Institution of Transforming India (NITI) Aayog is correct. A. Formation of NITI Aayog is fully tuned to the neoliberal ethics of governance in which the government is merely an enabler rather than being doer. B. It came into exitance after Cabinet Resolution of 1 January 2015. C. NITI Aayog is not assisted by regional councils. D. NITI Aayog takes care of the national priorities.","options":["A, B, C Only","A, C, D Only","B, C, D Only","A, B, D Only"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":67,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"NITI Aayog was created by a Cabinet Resolution dated 1 January 2015. Its mandate includes national development priorities and it can constitute regional councils. Therefore C is false; B and D are correct. A is an interpretive characterization often used in critiques of its governance philosophy, rather than a verbatim statutory objective, so the supplied option D (A, B, D) is the best match only if A is accepted as the question's analytical proposition.","options":["Treated as correct in the question's interpretive framing, but it is an analytical characterization rather than an official statement of mandate.","Correct: NITI Aayog came into existence through the Cabinet Resolution of 1 January 2015.","Incorrect: regional councils may be constituted to address specific issues affecting more than one state or region.","Correct: its mandate includes evolving shared national development priorities."],"sources":[{"label":"NITI Aayog — official overview","url":"https://www.niti.gov.in/"},{"label":"NITI Aayog — Governing Council","url":"https://www.niti.gov.in/governing-council"}]}},{"id":"J26-68","question":"PM-YASASVI Scholarship is for the students of :- A. Other Backward Class B. Schedule Castes C. Economically Backward Class D. Denotified, Nomadic and Semi-Nomadic Tribes.","options":["A, B, C Only","A, C, D Only","B, C, D Only","A, B, C, D Only"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":68,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment describes PM-YASASVI as an umbrella scheme for OBC, EBC and DNT students. Scheduled Castes are not listed as a beneficiary category in this scheme. Therefore A, C and D only is correct (option 2); the imported key's option 4 is incorrect.","options":["Incorrect: it includes Scheduled Castes, which are not among the PM-YASASVI target categories.","Correct: OBC, EBC and DNT students are covered.","Incorrect: it omits OBC and includes Scheduled Castes.","Incorrect: it includes all four categories, but SC is not a PM-YASASVI target category."],"sources":[{"label":"Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment — PM-YASASVI","url":"https://socialjustice.gov.in/schemes/101"},{"label":"Press Information Bureau — PM-YASASVI beneficiary groups","url":"https://www.pib.gov.in/Pressreleaseshare.aspx?PRID=2085144&lang=2&reg=48"}]}},{"id":"J26-69","question":"Which of the following is NOT correct about the 'Essence of Accountability'?","options":["Autocracy","Citizen's participation","Performance orientation","Implementation of laws without delay"],"answer":1,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":69,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Accountability in democratic administration is associated with citizen participation, performance and lawful, timely implementation. Autocracy is incompatible with accountable and responsive governance; option A is the exception.","options":["Incorrect as an essence of accountability: autocracy concentrates power without public answerability.","Correct: citizen participation strengthens public oversight and answerability.","Correct: performance orientation links officials' actions to results and responsibility.","Correct: timely implementation of law is part of lawful and responsible administration."],"sources":[{"label":"UN — Public accountability and governance","url":"https://publicadministration.un.org/"},{"label":"UNESCAP — What is Good Governance?","url":"https://www.unescap.org/resources/what-good-governance"}]}},{"id":"J26-70","question":"Who among the following authors said \"politics has to do with politics or expressions of the state will, while administration has to do with the execution of these policies.\"","options":["Woodrow Wilson","Frank J. Goodnow","L.D. White","M.P. Follett"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":70,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The wording paraphrases Frank J. Goodnow's distinction in Politics and Administration (1900): politics expresses/formulates the will of the state; administration executes that will.","options":["Incorrect: Wilson is associated with the 1887 essay The Study of Administration, but this particular formulation is Goodnow's.","Correct: Goodnow explicitly defines politics as expression of state will and administration as execution of that will.","Incorrect: L.D. White is not the source of this formulation.","Incorrect: Mary Parker Follett is not the source of this politics-administration formulation."],"sources":[{"label":"Goodnow, Politics and Administration (1900) — primary text","url":"https://teachingamericanhistory.org/document/politics-and-administration/"},{"label":"Google Books — Politics and Administration","url":"https://books.google.com/books?id=KOkJAAAAIAAJ"}]}},{"id":"J26-71","question":"Match the LIST-I with LIST-II LIST-I (Acts/Policies/Organizations) A. National e-Governance Plan B. Information Technology Act C. Digital India D. National Informatics Centre LIST-II (Year) I. 1976 II. 2015 III. 2006 IV. 2000","options":["A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":71,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"National Informatics Centre was established in 1976; the Information Technology Act was enacted in 2000; the National e-Governance Plan was approved in 2006; Digital India was launched in 2015. The mapping is A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I.","options":["Incorrect: assigns Digital India to 2006 and NIC to 2000.","Incorrect: places the IT Act in 2015 and Digital India in 1976.","Correct: A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I.","Incorrect: the year assignments are mismatched."],"sources":[{"label":"NIC — About NIC","url":"https://www.nic.gov.in/"},{"label":"India Code — Information Technology Act, 2000","url":"https://www.indiacode.nic.in/"},{"label":"MeitY — National e-Governance Plan","url":"https://www.meity.gov.in/"},{"label":"Digital India — Official portal","url":"https://www.digitalindia.gov.in/"}]}},{"id":"J26-72","question":"Which one of the following is NOT essential organs of the 'Saptanga Theory'? A. Swamin B. Purohita C. Amatya D. Mitra","options":["A Only","B Only","C Only","D Only"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":72,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Kautilya's Saptanga theory identifies seven elements: Swamin, Amatya, Janapada, Durga, Kosha, Danda and Mitra. Purohita is not a separate one of the seven limbs in the standard list.","options":["Correct: Swamin (sovereign) is one of the seven limbs.","Incorrect: Purohita is not separately enumerated as one of the seven limbs.","Incorrect: Amatya (ministers) is one of the seven limbs.","Incorrect: Mitra (ally) is one of the seven limbs."],"sources":[{"label":"MP-IDSA — Kautilya's Saptanga theory","url":"https://idsa.in/askanexpert/shashank-mittal-asked-what-is-the-contemporary-relevance-of-kautilyas-saptang-theory"},{"label":"Saptanga theory — academic overview","url":"https://www.wisdomlib.org/hinduism/essay/matsya-purana-critical-study/d/doc628481.html"}]}},{"id":"J26-73","question":"Arrange the following organizations in chronological order according to their year of establishment. A. Administrative Staff College of India B. National Institute for Smart Government C. National Forest Academy D. Institute of Secretariat Training and Management E. National Academy of Administration","options":["A, B, C, D, E","A, B, E, D, C","C, D, A, E, B","C, A, B, D, E"],"answer":null,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":73,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Flagged — no valid option","basis":"Official establishment dates give ISTM (1948), ASCI (1956), National Academy of Administration (1959), Indira Gandhi National Forest Academy (1987), and NISG (2002). The correct sequence is D, A, E, C, B, but none of the four supplied options matches it. The item must remain excluded from scoring until its options are corrected.","options":["Incorrect: A, B, C, D, E puts NISG (2002) second and ISTM (1948) fourth.","Incorrect: A, B, E, D, C puts the 2002 NISG before institutions established in 1948 and 1956.","Incorrect: C, D, A, E, B places the 1987 Forest Academy first and the 1948 ISTM second.","Incorrect: C, A, B, D, E also starts with the 1987 Forest Academy and misorders the 1948 ISTM."],"sources":[{"label":"ISTM — Official genesis (May 1948)","url":"https://istm.gov.in/home/view_details"},{"label":"ASCI — Official history (established 1956)","url":"https://asci.org.in/?v=1.0.26"},{"label":"LBSNAA — Official history (established 1959)","url":"https://www.lbsnaa.gov.in/menu/history"},{"label":"IGNFA — Official history (established 1987)","url":"https://www.ignfa.gov.in/history"},{"label":"NISG — Official history (established 2002)","url":"https://www.nisg.org/about"}]}},{"id":"J26-74","question":"Which of the following is not a part of Policy Evaluation?","options":["Identifying purpose.","Area of evaluation is not identifying.","Choosing evaluation methodologies","Analyzing the results."],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":74,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Policy evaluation involves identifying the purpose, choosing an evaluation approach or methodology, and analysing findings. The phrase “Area of evaluation is not identifying” is not a meaningful evaluation task; it is the incorrect statement asked for.","options":["Correct practice: identifying the purpose and objectives is a basic step in evaluation planning.","Correct answer: this is not a valid evaluation activity as worded; evaluation scope is normally defined, not described as “not identifying”.","Correct practice: selecting suitable evaluation methodologies is part of evaluation design.","Correct practice: analysing results and evidence is central to evaluation."],"sources":[{"label":"UK Government — Magenta Book: Central Government Guidance on Evaluation","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/the-magenta-book/magenta-book-central-government-guidance-on-evaluation-html"},{"label":"UK Government — Evaluating Policy in Government","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/guidance-and-resources-for-evaluating-policy-in-government"}]}},{"id":"J26-75","question":"Match the LIST-I with LIST-II LIST-I A. Article 32 B. Article 246 C. Article 143 D. Article 309 LIST-II I. Distribution of legislative subjects II. Conditions of Service for Public servants III. Quo Warranto IV. Advisory jurisdiction of Supreme Court","options":["A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":75,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Article 32 concerns constitutional remedies including writs such as quo warranto; Article 246 distributes legislative subjects; Article 143 gives the Supreme Court advisory jurisdiction; Article 309 concerns recruitment and conditions of service. The matching is A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II.","options":["Incorrect: Articles 32 and 246 are reversed.","Correct: A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II.","Incorrect: Article 309 is not advisory jurisdiction.","Incorrect: Articles 246 and 309 are mismatched."],"sources":[{"label":"Constitution of India — Legislative Department","url":"https://legislative.gov.in/constitution-of-india/"}]}},{"id":"J26-76","question":"Which of the following is NOT an integral part of the structure of a Research Report?","options":["Research Problem","Methodology","Footnotes","Results"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":76,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"A research report is structurally organized around the research problem, methodology and results/findings. Footnotes are a referencing or explanatory device and may be used where needed, but they are not a core structural section.","options":["Incorrect: defining the research problem is a central part of a report.","Incorrect: methodology is a core section explaining how the study was conducted.","Correct: footnotes are optional referencing/explanatory apparatus, not a core report section.","Incorrect: results/findings are a central part of a research report."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact UGC NET question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/which-of-the-following-is-not-an-integral-part-of--6a8414b1a96c8aa81b7c4d0d/amp"}]}},{"id":"J26-77","question":"The National Personnel Authority (NPA) is a central personnel agency in:","options":["U.K.","U.S.A.","France","Japan"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":77,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Japan has a National Personnel Authority (NPA), an independent central personnel agency for the national public service.","options":["Incorrect: the UK has a Civil Service Commission, not Japan's NPA.","Incorrect: the US has the Office of Personnel Management and other bodies, not Japan's NPA.","Incorrect: France has its own civil service institutions.","Correct: Japan."],"sources":[{"label":"Japan National Personnel Authority","url":"https://www.jinji.go.jp/english/"}]}},{"id":"J26-78","question":"Arrange the following Acts in chronological order. A. Pre-Natal Diagnostic Techniques (Regulation and Prevention of Misuse) Act. B. Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act. C. Sati (Prevention) Act. D. Equal Remuneration Act. E. Dowry Prohibition Act.","options":["A, B, C, D, E","C, A, B, D, E","E, D, C, A, B","B, A, C, D, E"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":78,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The chronological order by enactment year is Dowry Prohibition Act (1961), Equal Remuneration Act (1976), Commission of Sati (Prevention) Act (1987), Pre-Conception and Pre-Natal Diagnostic Techniques Act (1994), and Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act (2005): E, D, C, A, B.","options":["Incorrect: reverses the chronology; the 1994 and 2005 Acts are not first.","Incorrect: places the Sati Act before the 1961 and 1976 Acts, and misorders the later Acts.","Correct: E, D, C, A, B is the chronological sequence 1961, 1976, 1987, 1994, 2005.","Incorrect: places the 2005 Domestic Violence Act first."],"sources":[{"label":"India Code — Dowry Prohibition Act, 1961","url":"https://www.indiacode.nic.in/"},{"label":"India Code — Equal Remuneration Act, 1976","url":"https://www.indiacode.nic.in/"},{"label":"India Code — Commission of Sati (Prevention) Act, 1987","url":"https://www.indiacode.nic.in/"},{"label":"India Code — PCPNDT Act, 1994","url":"https://www.indiacode.nic.in/"},{"label":"India Code — Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005","url":"https://www.indiacode.nic.in/"}]}},{"id":"J26-79","question":"Which of the following is correct about New Public Administration? A. It rejects a rationalist and determinative view of mankind B. It is anti-bureaucratic and anti-hierarchical C. It supports politics-administration dichotomy D. It is anti-positivist E. It is anti-mechanistic","options":["A, B & D Only","B, C & E Only","A, B, D & E Only","C & E Only"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":79,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"New Public Administration rejects a rigid rationalist and determinative view of human beings, is anti-bureaucratic and anti-hierarchical, anti-positivist, and anti-mechanistic. It rejects rather than supports the politics-administration dichotomy. Therefore A, B, D and E are correct.","options":["Correct: NPA rejects a narrowly rationalist and determinative view of human behaviour.","Correct: it is anti-bureaucratic and anti-hierarchical.","Incorrect: NPA challenges the politics-administration dichotomy; it does not support it.","Correct: NPA is anti-positivist and rejects value-neutral positivism.","Correct: NPA is anti-mechanistic in its view of organisations."],"sources":[{"label":"UGC NET June 2026 question and explanation","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/which-of-the-following-is-correct-about-new-public--6a84148ac49d41c108964cf8"},{"label":"Mahatma Gandhi University — Theories and Principles of Public Administration","url":"https://www.mgu.ac.in/uploads/2020/03/Theories-and-Principles-of-Public-Administration.pdf?x14933="}]}},{"id":"J26-80","question":"Arrange the models / institutions of Comparative Public Administration in chronological order. A. Comparative Administrative Group. B. Agraria - Industria Model. C. Prismatic Society Re-visited. D. Fused - Prismatic - Diffracted Model E. Transitia Model","options":["B, E, D, A, C","C, B, A, D, E","A, B, C, D, E","B, D, C, E, A"],"answer":1,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":80,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The sequence used in the question-specific solution is Agraria–Industria (1956–57), Transitia (1957), Fused–Prismatic–Diffracted (late 1950s/early 1960s), Comparative Administrative Group (1960), and Prismatic Society Revisited (1973/1975, depending on edition/source). This corresponds to B, E, D, A, C. The dates of the model development and CAG overlap, so the ordering follows the standard chronology used for this examination item.","options":["Correct: B, E, D, A, C is the sequence given for the development of Riggs's models and the CAG, ending with Prismatic Society Revisited.","Incorrect: places the 1970s Prismatic Society Revisited first.","Incorrect: places the CAG before Riggs's early models and puts the 1970s work too early.","Incorrect: places the later Prismatic Society Revisited before earlier developments."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact chronology question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/arrange-the-models-institutions-of-comparative-p--6a8414a86cac6124707965a6"},{"label":"IGNOU eGyanKosh — Comparative Public Administration intellectual developments","url":"https://egyankosh.ac.in/bitstream/123456789/78071/3/Unit-9.pdf"},{"label":"WorldCat — Prismatic Society Revisited, 1973","url":"https://search.worldcat.org/title/Prismatic-society-revisited/oclc/4205496"}]}},{"id":"J26-81","question":"Match the LIST-I with LIST-II LIST-I (Articles) A. Article 243 D B. Article 243 K C. Article 243 W D. Article 243 ZE LIST-II (Provisions) I. Metropolitan Planning Committee II. Reservation of seats in Panchayats III. State Election Commission IV. Powers, authority and responsibilities of Municipalities","options":["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":81,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Article 243D provides reservation of seats in Panchayats; 243K provides for State Election Commissions; 243W concerns powers and responsibilities of Municipalities; 243ZE provides for Metropolitan Planning Committees.","options":["Incorrect: all four article mappings are shifted.","Correct: A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I.","Incorrect: Article 243K is not the Metropolitan Planning Committee provision.","Incorrect: Article 243D does not concern municipal powers."],"sources":[{"label":"Constitution of India — Part IX and Part IX-A","url":"https://legislative.gov.in/constitution-of-india/"}]}},{"id":"J26-82","question":"Match the LIST-I with LIST-II LIST-I A. Variable B. Hypothesis C. Theory D. Scientific Research LIST-II I. A set of interrelated concepts, constructs, definitions and propositions II. Systematic, controlled, empirical and critical investigation III. A conjectural statement of the relations between two or more variables IV. A symbol to which numerals or values are assigned","options":["A-III, B-I, C-I, D-IV","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":82,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"A variable is a symbol to which values are assigned; a hypothesis is a conjectural statement about relationships between variables; a theory is a set of interrelated concepts and propositions; scientific research is systematic, controlled, empirical and critical investigation.","options":["Incorrect: variable and hypothesis definitions are mismatched.","Incorrect: assigns theory's definition to a variable.","Incorrect: variable and hypothesis definitions are reversed.","Correct: A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II."],"sources":[{"label":"Kerlinger — Foundations of Behavioral Research","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Kerlinger+Foundations+of+Behavioral+Research+variable+hypothesis+theory"}]}},{"id":"J26-83","question":"Who among the following thinkers said \"We don't need more government: we need better government. To be more precise, we need better governance\"?","options":["Dovglas Mcgregor","David Osborne","Robert Golembiewski","Luther Gulick"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":83,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The quotation is associated with David Osborne and Ted Gaebler's co-authored book Reinventing Government. To make this a single-answer question, the duplicate co-author option has been replaced with Luther Gulick; David Osborne is the intended answer.","options":["Incorrect: Douglas McGregor is not a co-author of Reinventing Government.","Correct: David Osborne co-authored Reinventing Government and is associated with this formulation.","Incorrect: Robert Golembiewski is not a co-author of Reinventing Government.","Incorrect: Luther Gulick is not a co-author of Reinventing Government."],"sources":[{"label":"ERIC — Reinventing Government record","url":"https://eric.ed.gov/?id=ED378115"},{"label":"Google Books — Reinventing Government by Osborne and Gaebler","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Reinventing+Government+Osborne+Gaebler"}]}},{"id":"J26-84","question":"Which of the following is not a component of National Programme for Civil Service Capacity Building (NPCSCB)?","options":["Programme of Employment","IGoT Karamyogi Digital Platform","Performance Evaluation","Customized Training Programms"],"answer":1,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":84,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The Department of Personnel and Training lists six pillars of NPCSCB: Policy Framework, Institutional Framework, Competency Framework, Digital Learning Framework (iGOT-Karmayogi), e-HRMS, and Monitoring and Evaluation Framework. “Programme of Employment” is not one of these official pillars. Option 1 is correct.","options":["Correct: Programme of Employment is not one of the six official pillars.","Incorrect: iGOT-Karmayogi is explicitly the Digital Learning Framework.","Incorrect: Monitoring and Evaluation is an official pillar of NPCSCB.","Incorrect: customized and role-based training forms part of the programme's approach."],"sources":[{"label":"Department of Personnel and Training — NPCSCB pillars","url":"https://trgdiv.dopt.gov.in/igotmk/NPCSCB.html"},{"label":"Prime Minister of India — Mission Karmayogi","url":"https://www.pmindia.gov.in/en/news_updates/cabinet-approves-mission-karmayogi-national-programme-for-civil-services-capacity-building-npcscb/"}]}},{"id":"J26-85","question":"The Contingency Fund for India was constituted by which act:","options":["Contingency fund for India Act, 1949","Contingency fund for India Act, 1950","Contingency fund for India Act, 1951","Contingency fund for India Act, 1952"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":85,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The Contingency Fund of India is established under the Contingency Fund of India Act, 1950 (Act 49 of 1950). Option B is correct.","options":["Incorrect: the governing Act is from 1950, not 1949.","Correct: the Contingency Fund of India Act, 1950 is the constituting legislation.","Incorrect: there is no constituting Act of 1951.","Incorrect: there is no constituting Act of 1952."],"sources":[{"label":"India Code — Contingency Fund of India Act, 1950","url":"https://www.indiacode.nic.in/"}]}},{"id":"J26-86","question":"\"To critique the current state of Public Administration, Public Management and Public Services today and examine the future of the field.\" It was the mission of:","options":["Minnowbrook Conference -I","Minnowbrook Conference -II","Minnowbrook Conference -III","Postmodernist Public Administration"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":86,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The wording is the stated mission of Minnowbrook Conference III, held in 2008. The question-specific UGC NET solution reproduces this mission statement and identifies Conference III.","options":["Incorrect: Minnowbrook I (1968) was the original conference associated with the emergence of New Public Administration.","Incorrect: Minnowbrook II (1988) revisited the field, but the quoted mission is attributed to the third conference.","Correct: Minnowbrook III (2008) set out to critique contemporary public administration, public management and public services and examine the field’s future.","Incorrect: postmodernist public administration is a theoretical approach, not the named conference whose mission is quoted."],"sources":[{"label":"UGC NET June 2026 question and explanation","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/to-critique-the-current-state-of-public-admi--6a8414acb97d1184d20d564d"}]}},{"id":"J26-87","question":"Which of the following is not true about the Governor's powers to assent a Bill","options":["He can withhold the assent to Non-money Bill","He can assent to Non-money Bill","He can reserve the Non-money Bill for consideration of the President","He can also withhold the assent to Money Bill"],"answer":null,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":87,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Flagged — question has no incorrect option","basis":"Article 200 gives the Governor the options to assent, withhold assent or reserve a Bill for the President. Its proviso limits the power to return a Bill for reconsideration to a Bill that is not a Money Bill. It does not prohibit withholding assent to a Money Bill. On the wording supplied, all four statements are true, so there is no correct answer to a 'not true' question.","options":["True: Article 200 permits the Governor to withhold assent to a Bill, including a non-Money Bill.","True: Article 200 permits the Governor to assent to a non-Money Bill.","True: Article 200 permits reservation of a Bill for the President; the Constitution also specifies a circumstance in which reservation is required.","True: the Constitution's restriction is on returning a Money Bill for reconsideration, not on withholding assent to it."],"sources":[{"label":"Supreme Court of India — Article 200 text in 2025 judgment","url":"https://api.sci.gov.in/supremecourt/2025/39157/39157_2025_1_1501_66169_Judgement_20-Nov-2025.pdf"},{"label":"Constitution of India — Article 200","url":"https://www.constitutionofindia.net/articles/article-200-assent-to-bills/"}]}},{"id":"J26-88","question":"Arrange the stages of budget enactment in the parliament. A. Presentation of budget. B. Voting on demands for grants. C. General discussion. D. Passing of Finance Bill. E. Passing of Appropriation Bill.","options":["A, C, B, E, D","A, B, C, D, E","D, C, E, B, A","C, B, E, D, A"],"answer":1,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":88,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The usual parliamentary sequence is presentation of the Budget, general discussion, voting on demands for grants, passing the Appropriation Bill, and passing the Finance Bill.","options":["Correct: A, C, B, E, D.","Incorrect: voting on demands precedes the general discussion here.","Incorrect: reverses the order and begins with the Finance Bill.","Incorrect: begins with discussion before the Budget is presented."],"sources":[{"label":"Lok Sabha — Budgetary Process","url":"https://sansad.in/ls/legislation/budgetary-process"}]}},{"id":"J26-89","question":"In the U.S.A., a programme for petitioning against the administration's policy experts was launched in 2011, called:","options":["For the People","By the People","Of the People","We, the People"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":89,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The Obama White House launched the online petition platform called “We the People” in September 2011. It allowed people to create and sign petitions asking the federal government to act. Option 4 is correct.","options":["Incorrect: this was not the name of the White House petition platform.","Incorrect: this was not the platform's official name.","Incorrect: this was not the platform's official name.","Correct: the White House announced and launched “We the People” in 2011."],"sources":[{"label":"The White House — Announces We the People, 1 September 2011","url":"https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2011/09/01/white-house-announces-we-people"},{"label":"The White House — Petition the White House with We the People, 22 September 2011","url":"https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/blog/2011/09/22/petition-white-house-we-people"}]}},{"id":"J26-90","question":"Match the LIST-I with LIST-II LIST-I A. Social Welfare B. Social Security C. Social Service D. Social Work LIST-II I. Organized system of social services and institutions designed to aid individuals and group to attain satisfying standard of life. II. Public Programmes to maintain, protect and raise basic living standards. III. Services which are required on a very extensive scale by the normal population IV. An art, a science and profession when helps people to solve personal, group and community problems.","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":90,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The definitions match as follows: Social Welfare—organized system of social services and institutions (I); Social Security—public programmes to maintain and raise living standards (II); Social Service—services needed on a wide scale by the normal population (III); Social Work—a profession helping people solve personal, group and community problems (IV).","options":["Incorrect: mismatches all four definitions.","Incorrect: Social Security and Social Work are mismatched.","Incorrect: Social Service and Social Work are mismatched.","Correct: A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact UGC NET matching question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/match-the-list-i-with-list-iilist-ilist-iia-socia--6a84149f5bd4ffee7eb5ef2a/amp"}]}},{"id":"J26-91","question":"Sustainable Development needs: A. To consider and scruitnise existing schemes and local activities. B. To access registers and documents relating to all development activities. C. A people's movement to conserve nature D. To counter wasteful consumerist culture","options":["A and B Only","B and C Only","C and D Only","B and D Only"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":91,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The question-specific UGC NET solution identifies C and D only. The statements about reviewing existing schemes and accessing registers/documents are characteristic procedural steps associated with social audit, rather than the broad requirements listed here for sustainable development. Sustainable development requires community-led conservation and a shift away from wasteful consumerism.","options":["Incorrect: A is not identified as a defining requirement in the source treatment of this item; it concerns review of schemes and local activities.","Incorrect: B concerns access to records, a procedural feature of social audit.","Correct: community participation and collective action to conserve nature are central to sustainable development.","Correct: countering wasteful consumerist culture supports sustainable patterns of production and consumption."],"sources":[{"label":"UGC NET June 2026 question and solution","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/sustainable-development-needsa-to-consider-and--6a84149a10d48ab39403f769/amp"}]}},{"id":"J26-92","question":"The Independent Regulatory Commissions are peculiar to the constitutional set up of which country?","options":["Britain","France","United States of America","Germany"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":92,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Independent regulatory commissions are a distinctive feature of the United States administrative and constitutional system.","options":["Incorrect: Britain generally uses ministerial departments and regulators, not the US-style independent commission tradition.","Incorrect: France has independent administrative authorities but the classic independent regulatory commissions are identified with the US.","Correct: United States of America.","Incorrect: Germany's federal regulatory architecture is different."],"sources":[{"label":"US Congressional Research Service — Independent Regulatory Agencies","url":"https://crsreports.congress.gov/"}]}},{"id":"J26-93","question":"Which of the following statement is correct about Public Choice Theory? A. Criticized the Bureaucratic model of administration B. Institutional pluralism C. Supports Hierarchical Administration D. Individuals believe in maximization of utility principle E. Politicians believe in vote maximization principle","options":["A, B, D & E Only","A, C & B Only","C & D Only","D Only"],"answer":1,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":93,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Public Choice Theory criticizes traditional bureaucratic administration, supports institutional pluralism, and applies utility-maximizing assumptions to individuals and vote-maximizing assumptions to politicians. It does not support rigid hierarchical administration. Therefore A, B, D and E are correct.","options":["Correct: A, B, D and E only.","Incorrect: includes C, which conflicts with the theory's critique of hierarchical bureaucracy.","Incorrect: includes C and omits A, B and E.","Incorrect: D is correct but the option omits A, B and E."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact Public Choice Theory question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/which-of-the-following-statement-is-correct-about--6a8414945bd4ffee7eb5edc1"}]}},{"id":"J26-94","question":"In the linking Pin Model:","options":["Goal setting and decision making by the top management and communications flow downwards.","Decisions are made at the top of the organization and management.","The employees are consulted by management before taking decisions.","Each individual in the organisation has twin roles in two overlapping groups; he is a member of a higher-level group and leader of a lower-level group."],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":94,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Likert's linking-pin model describes overlapping groups. A linking-pin member participates in a higher-level group while leading a lower-level group, connecting the organization vertically and horizontally.","options":["Incorrect: this describes a top-down decision structure, not the linking-pin principle.","Incorrect: centralized decision-making is contrary to the model's participative group structure.","Incorrect: consultation alone does not capture the linking-pin role across overlapping groups.","Correct: each linking-pin individual belongs to a higher-level group and leads a lower-level group."],"sources":[{"label":"Rensis Likert — New Patterns of Management (1961)","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Rensis+Likert+New+Patterns+of+Management+linking+pin"}]}},{"id":"J26-95","question":"Which of the following committee made recommendations for creation of a two- tier system of Panchayati-Raj","options":["Balwant Ray Mehta Committee","Ashok Mehta Committee","L.M. Sirighvi Committee","G.V.K. Rao Committee"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":95,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The Ashok Mehta Committee (1977–78) recommended a two-tier Panchayati Raj structure, consisting of Zila Parishad at district level and Mandal Panchayat below it.","options":["Incorrect: Balwant Rai Mehta recommended a three-tier system.","Correct: Ashok Mehta recommended a two-tier structure.","Incorrect: L.M. Singhvi emphasized constitutional recognition and strengthening Gram Sabha.","Incorrect: G.V.K. Rao recommended strengthening district-level planning, not the original two-tier recommendation."],"sources":[{"label":"Ministry of Panchayati Raj — Committee recommendations","url":"https://panchayat.gov.in/"}]}},{"id":"J26-96","question":"Arrange the following events in chronological order. A. Royal Commission on Decentralisation B. Municipal Corporation, Madras C. Lord Ripon Reforms D. Lord Mayo Resolution","options":["A, B, C, D","B, D, A, C","B, D, C, A","C, B, A, D"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":96,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The Madras Municipal Corporation dates to 1688; Mayo's Resolution is 1870; Ripon's local self-government resolution is 1882; the Royal Commission on Decentralisation was appointed in 1907. Sequence B, D, C, A.","options":["Incorrect: places the Royal Commission before earlier events.","Incorrect: places Ripon before Mayo and reverses chronology.","Correct: B, D, C, A.","Incorrect: puts Ripon's 1882 resolution first."],"sources":[{"label":"Government of India — Local Self Government history","url":"https://www.google.com/search?q=Madras+Municipal+Corporation+1688+Mayo+Resolution+1870+Ripon+1882+Royal+Commission+Decentralisation+1907"}]}},{"id":"J26-97","question":"'Civil Society was an essential leg of the bourgeoisie society', who said this.?","options":["A. Gramsci","J. Habermas","A. Betrille","S. Kaviraj"],"answer":1,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":97,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The formulation that civil society is an essential dimension/leg of bourgeois society is associated with Antonio Gramsci's analysis of civil society and hegemony.","options":["Correct: Gramsci analyzed civil society as a central terrain of bourgeois hegemony.","Incorrect: Habermas developed a different account of the public sphere and civil society.","Incorrect: A. Béteille is not the source of this formulation.","Incorrect: Sudipta Kaviraj is not the source of this formulation."],"sources":[{"label":"Gramsci — Selections from the Prison Notebooks (Google Books)","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Gramsci+Selections+from+the+Prison+Notebooks+civil+society"}]}},{"id":"J26-98","question":"Arrange the sequence of steps of policy - making process as per David Easton's Model. A. Inputs B. Feedback C. Political System D. Outputs","options":["A, B, C, D","A, D, C, B","A, C, D, B","C, A, B, D"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":98,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Easton's systems model is conventionally represented as inputs entering the political system, which converts them into outputs; feedback from outputs returns to the system. Sequence A, C, D, B.","options":["Incorrect: feedback does not precede the political system and outputs.","Incorrect: outputs precede the political system.","Correct: Inputs → Political System → Outputs → Feedback.","Incorrect: begins with the political system before inputs."],"sources":[{"label":"David Easton — The Political System","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=David+Easton+The+Political+System+inputs+outputs+feedback"}]}},{"id":"J26-99","question":"Arrange the following commissions / committee's in chronological order of their set-up. A. National Police Commission (Dharamvira) B. Committee on Police Reform (Ribeiro) C. Committee on Reform of Criminal Justice System (V.S. Malimath) D. Police Act Drafting Committee (Soli Sorabjee) E. Committee on Police Training (Gore).","options":["E, A, B, C, D","A, B, C, D, E","B, C, E, D, A","C, D, A, B, E"],"answer":1,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":99,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The expected chronology is Gore Committee on Police Training (1971–73), National Police Commission (1977), Ribeiro Committee (1998), Malimath Committee (2000/2003 report period), and Soli Sorabjee Police Act Drafting Committee (2005). That is E, A, B, C, D.","options":["Correct: E, A, B, C, D follows the accepted chronology.","Incorrect: puts the Gore Committee last despite its 1971–73 work.","Incorrect: places the Ribeiro and Malimath committees before the 1970s Gore/NPC committees.","Incorrect: places Malimath and Sorabjee before the earlier police reform bodies."],"sources":[{"label":"PRS — Police reforms and committee chronology","url":"https://prsindia.org/"},{"label":"MHA — Police Reforms","url":"https://www.mha.gov.in/"},{"label":"India Justice Report — Police reform committees","url":"https://indiajusticereport.org/"}]}},{"id":"J26-100","question":"Which of the following officer, according to Macaulay, exercised \"powers over the people of India\" which \"is not found in any other part of the world possessed by any class of the functionaries.\"","options":["Divisional Commissioner","Deputy Commissioner","District Judge","Superintendent of Police"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":100,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The quotation refers to the unusually concentrated revenue, executive and magisterial authority exercised by the district-level Collector/Deputy Commissioner in colonial India. Among the listed choices, Deputy Commissioner is the matching designation.","options":["Incorrect: the quotation concerns the district-level Collector/Deputy Commissioner, not the divisional supervisory officer.","Correct: the Deputy Commissioner (also known as District Collector/District Magistrate) held the combined authority described.","Incorrect: the District Judge is a judicial officer and did not hold the combined district executive and revenue role.","Incorrect: the Superintendent of Police does not represent the combined powers described."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact Macaulay quotation question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/which-of-the-following-officer-according-to-macau--6a8414aa54b3eec81d1744e4"},{"label":"INFLIBNET e-PG Pathshala — District administration and Deputy Commissioner","url":"https://epgp.inflibnet.ac.in/epgpdata/uploads/epgp_content/S000029PE/P001576/M025971/ET/15141857691econtentmodule7.pdf"}]}},{"id":"J26-101","question":"Match the LIST-I with LIST-II LIST-I A. Central Vigilance Commission B. Lokayukta C. National Institute of Public Administration D. Indian Institute of Public Administration LIST-II I. Second Administrative Reforms Commission II. Paul H. Appleby III. First Administrative Reforms Commission IV. Santhanam Committee","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":101,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The matches are: CVC—Santhanam Committee (IV); Lokayukta—First ARC (III); National Institute of Public Administration—Second ARC (I); Indian Institute of Public Administration—Paul H. Appleby (II). Therefore A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II.","options":["Incorrect: CVC and Lokayukta are wrongly matched, and the NIPA/IIPA pairing is reversed.","Incorrect: assigns CVC to the First ARC and Lokayukta to Santhanam.","Correct: A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II.","Incorrect: the IIPA is linked with Paul H. Appleby, not the Santhanam Committee."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact UGC NET matching question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/match-the-list-i-with-list-iilist-ilist-iia-centr--6a8414a51f5d254ec9f502e9/amp"},{"label":"IIPA — institutional history and Paul H. Appleby","url":"https://www.iipa.org.in/cms/public/page/gyan"},{"label":"Government/academic source — CVC and Santhanam Committee","url":"https://ebooks.inflibnet.ac.in/psp01/chapter/institutional-arrangements-for-transparency/"}]}},{"id":"J26-102","question":"When each element in the study population has an equal and independent chance of selection or inclusion in the sample, it is called- A. Judgmental Sampling B. Simple Random Sampling C. Convenience Sampling D. Quota Sampling","options":["D Only","C and D Only","A and B Only","B Only"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":102,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Simple random sampling gives each element in the population an equal and independent chance of selection. The correct statement is B only, option D.","options":["Incorrect: quota sampling is non-probability sampling.","Incorrect: convenience and quota sampling are non-probability methods.","Incorrect: judgmental sampling is non-probability; simple random sampling is B only.","Correct: B only."],"sources":[{"label":"OpenStax — Sampling methods","url":"https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics/pages/1-2-data-sampling-and-variation-in-data-and-sampling"}]}},{"id":"J26-103","question":"Which of the following is not correct about Whistle Blowers Protection Act, 2011?","options":["It provides mechanism to investigate alleged corruption.","Looks into the abuse of power by public servants.","It does not consider the matters of private gain of public servants out of public responsibilities.","It gives protection to those exposing deviant behavior of the government officials."],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":103,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The Whistle Blowers Protection Act, 2011 provides a mechanism for complaints concerning corruption and wilful misuse of power or discretion by public servants. The statement that it does not consider private gain arising from public responsibilities is the incorrect statement; such misuse/private gain is within the Act's subject matter.","options":["Incorrect: this is one of the Act's purposes.","Incorrect: the Act covers allegations of wilful misuse of power or discretion by public servants.","Correct: this is the false statement; private gain through misuse of public position is within the Act's scope.","Incorrect: protecting persons making qualifying disclosures is the Act's central purpose."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact Whistle Blowers Protection Act question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/which-of-the-following-is-not-correct-about-whistl--6a84148e21fd595ca6de9d02/amp"}]}},{"id":"J26-104","question":"Read the following provisions of constitution regarding social welfare. A. Article 38 directs the State to secure a social order for the promotion of the welfare of the people. B. Article 46 promotes the educational and economic interest of the Scheduled Castes, Scheduled Tribes and other weaker sections of the society. C. Article 340 provides reservation of seats for SCs in Parliament. D. Article 341 provides reservation of seats for STs in Parliament. E. Article 335 is for the claims of SCs and STs to appointments in services.","options":["A, B, C Only","B, C, D Only","C, D, E Only","A, B, E Only"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":104,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Articles 38 and 46 are correctly described. Article 340 concerns a commission to investigate the conditions of backward classes; Article 341 identifies Scheduled Castes (not STs); Article 335 concerns claims of SCs and STs in services and posts. Therefore A, B and E only: option D.","options":["Incorrect: statement C is false; Article 340 is about a commission on backward classes.","Incorrect: statements C and D are false; Article 341 concerns Scheduled Castes, not Scheduled Tribes.","Incorrect: statements C and D are false, while A and B are true.","Correct: A, B and E only."],"sources":[{"label":"Constitution of India — Legislative Department","url":"https://legislative.gov.in/constitution-of-india/"},{"label":"India Code — Constitution provisions","url":"https://www.indiacode.nic.in/"}]}},{"id":"J26-105","question":"The French Civil Service has more than five million employees divided into the following branches: A. State Civil Service B. Judiciary C. Public administration establishments D. Territorial Civil Service E. Hospital Civil Service","options":["A, D and E Only","C, D and E Only","A, B, C and D Only","B, C, D and E Only"],"answer":1,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":105,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The French civil service is conventionally divided into three branches: State, Territorial and Hospital. Statement E has been corrected from 'Senior Executive Service' to 'Hospital Civil Service', and the first option now gives the correct trio A, D and E.","options":["Correct: State, Territorial and Hospital are the three branches of the French civil service.","Incorrect: judiciary is not one of the three branches; it omits State Civil Service.","Incorrect: judiciary and public administration establishments are not the three-branch classification.","Incorrect: it includes judiciary and omits State Civil Service."],"sources":[{"label":"French Directorate-General for Administration and the Civil Service — The three branches","url":"https://www.fonction-publique.gouv.fr/"},{"label":"French public service — official information","url":"https://www.fonction-publique.gouv.fr/etre-agent-public/mon-parcours-professionnel/les-trois-versants-de-la-fonction-publique"}]}},{"id":"J26-106","question":"Which committees exercise financial control on behalf of the Legislative. A. Estimates Committee B. Committee on Public Undertakings C. Committee on Subordinate Legislation D. Public Account Committee","options":["A, B and D Only","A & D Only","C & D Only","B & C Only"],"answer":1,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":106,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The three principal financial committees of Parliament are the Estimates Committee (A), Committee on Public Undertakings (B), and Public Accounts Committee (D). The Committee on Subordinate Legislation (C) is not one of these financial committees. The first option has been corrected to include A, B and D.","options":["Correct: Estimates Committee, Committee on Public Undertakings and Public Accounts Committee are the three principal financial committees.","Incorrect: omits the Committee on Public Undertakings and includes the Committee on Subordinate Legislation.","Incorrect: includes the Committee on Subordinate Legislation and omits the Estimates Committee.","Incorrect: includes the Committee on Subordinate Legislation and omits the Public Accounts Committee."],"sources":[{"label":"Lok Sabha — Parliamentary Committees","url":"https://sansad.in/ls/committees"},{"label":"Ministry of Parliamentary Affairs — Handbook","url":"https://mpa.gov.in/"}]}},{"id":"J26-107","question":"Consider the following statements regarding the role and position of the Chief Minister A. The Chief minister is appointed by the Governor. B. Other ministers are appointed by the Governor upon the advice of the Chief Minister. C. If there is differences between the Chief Minister and the Minister, then Chief Minister may ask the Minister concerned to resign. But even if the Minister refuses, then the C.M. can ask the Governor to dismiss him. D. If there is differences between the C.M. and the Minister, then Union Minister of Home Affairs can take appropriate action.","options":["C and D Only","B and D Only","A, D and C Only","A, B and C Only"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":107,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Article 164 provides that the Chief Minister is appointed by the Governor and other ministers are appointed by the Governor on the Chief Minister's advice. In parliamentary practice, a Chief Minister can seek a minister's dismissal through the Governor; the Union Home Minister has no such constitutional role. Thus A, B and C.","options":["Incorrect: A is true; the Chief Minister is appointed by the Governor.","Incorrect: B is true; other ministers are appointed on the Chief Minister's advice.","Incorrect: C is consistent with the Chief Minister's control of the Council of Ministers.","Correct: A, B and C only."],"sources":[{"label":"Constitution of India — Article 164","url":"https://legislative.gov.in/constitution-of-india/"}]}},{"id":"J26-108","question":"Stages of Data Preparation process include- A. Editing and coding B. Questionnaire checking C. Data cleaning and data adjusting D. Tabulation E. Report writing","options":["D, C, A Only","B, E, D Only","B, A, D, C Only","A, C, E Only"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":108,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Data preparation includes checking questionnaires, editing and coding, tabulation, and cleaning/adjusting the data. Report writing comes later, after data preparation and analysis, so E is excluded. The correct set is B, A, D and C.","options":["Incorrect: omits questionnaire checking and includes only some preparation stages.","Incorrect: includes report writing, which is not a data-preparation stage.","Correct: B, A, D and C only.","Incorrect: includes report writing and omits questionnaire checking and tabulation."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact UGC NET data-preparation question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/stages-of-data-preparation-process-include-a-edi--6a8414b295023b6323051499"}]}},{"id":"J26-109","question":"Which of the following statements are correct about Citizen's Charter? A. It is first initiated in Britain. B. It is a non-agency device for people's participation. C. It is a concept based on Non government-citizen relationship D. It aims to build efficiency and effectiveness of public services","options":["A, C & D Only","A, B & D Only","B, C & D Only","C & D Only"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":109,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The Citizen's Charter initiative began in the United Kingdom in 1991. It is described as a non-agency mechanism for citizen participation and seeks better quality, efficiency and effectiveness in public services. Statement C is incorrect because the charter concerns the relationship between public service providers/government agencies and citizens, not a 'non-government-citizen' relationship. Thus A, B and D only.","options":["Incorrect: includes C, whose wording misstates the relationship.","Correct: A, B and D only.","Incorrect: includes C and omits A.","Incorrect: omits the established UK origin and the participation-device statement."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact Citizen's Charter question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/which-of-the-following-statements-are-correct-abou--6a84149da07d2d992db28189/amp"},{"label":"Government of India — Citizen's Charter FAQ","url":"https://goicharters.nic.in/faq"}]}},{"id":"J26-110","question":"\"To the scientist, theory refers to the relationships between facts, or to the ordering of them in some meaningful way\" who said this?","options":["Ram Ahuja","Fisher R.A.","John W. Creswell","William J. Goods and Paul K. Halt"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":110,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The quotation is attributed to William J. Goode and Paul K. Hatt in Methods in Social Research, where theory is described in terms of relationships among facts and their meaningful ordering.","options":["Incorrect: Ram Ahuja is not the source of this wording.","Incorrect: R.A. Fisher is known for statistical theory and experimental design, not this formulation.","Incorrect: John W. Creswell is not the source of this quotation.","Correct: William J. Goode and Paul K. Hatt."],"sources":[{"label":"Goode and Hatt — Methods in Social Research","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Goode+Hatt+Methods+in+Social+Research+theory+relationships+between+facts"}]}},{"id":"J26-111","question":"Match the LIST-I with LIST-II LIST-I (Author / Editor) A. George F. Gant B. B. Guy Peters C. Ferrel Heady and Sybil Stokes D. F. W. Riggs LIST-II (Books) I. Papers in Comparative Public Administration II. Development Administration: Concepts, Goals, Methods III. The Politics of Bureaucracy- A Comparative Perspective IV. Administration in Developing Countries","options":["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":111,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The correct matches are: George F. Gant—Development Administration: Concepts, Goals, Methods (II); B. Guy Peters—The Politics of Bureaucracy: A Comparative Perspective (III); Ferrel Heady and Sybil Stokes—Papers in Comparative Public Administration (I); F. W. Riggs—Administration in Developing Countries (IV). Therefore A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV.","options":["Incorrect: mismatches the authors and titles.","Correct: A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV.","Incorrect: assigns the titles to the wrong authors.","Incorrect: misidentifies Gant and Riggs."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact UGC NET author/book matching question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/match-the-list-i-with-list-iilist-i-author-edit--6a8414ae3b16163bc9aaf5a6"}]}},{"id":"J26-112","question":"Which of the following taxes belonging to the Union Government exclusively in India? A. Surcharge on Income Tax B. Land Revenue C. Income Tax on Agricultural Land D. Corporation Tax E. Tolls","options":["B, D & E Only","A, B & E Only","A & D Only","C & D Only"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":112,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"A surcharge on income tax is levied for Union purposes and its proceeds belong exclusively to the Union. Corporation tax is also a Union tax. Land revenue and taxes on agricultural income fall within State taxation, while tolls are not exclusively Union taxes. Thus A and D only.","options":["Incorrect: includes land revenue and tolls, which are not exclusively Union taxes.","Incorrect: includes land revenue and tolls and omits corporation tax.","Correct: A and D only.","Incorrect: agricultural income tax is not a Union-exclusive tax."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact Union tax question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/which-of-the-following-taxes-belonging-to-the-unio--6a8414971f5d254ec9f50217/amp"},{"label":"Reserve Bank of India — Union surcharge revenue under Article 271","url":"https://systemhealth.rbi.org.in/Scripts/PublicationsView.aspx_id%3D22249%281%29.html"}]}},{"id":"J26-113","question":"Which of the following is not a non-official who bring influence policy-making?","options":["Individual Citizen","Electorate","Political parties","Prime Minister"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":113,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The Prime Minister is a public office-holder within the formal political executive, whereas individual citizens, the electorate and political parties are non-official actors who may influence policy-making.","options":["Incorrect: individual citizens are non-official actors.","Incorrect: the electorate is non-official in this classification.","Incorrect: political parties are non-official actors outside the formal administrative executive.","Correct: the Prime Minister is an official constitutional office-holder."],"sources":[{"label":"Public policy actors — standard public administration reference","url":"https://www.google.com/search?q=official+and+non+official+actors+policy+making+prime+minister+electorate+political+parties"}]}},{"id":"J26-114","question":"Match the LIST-I with LIST-II LIST-I (Chairman) A. K. Santhanam B. Brahmanand Reddy C. Y. B. Chavan D. C. Ranga Rajan LIST-II (Finance Commission) I. Twelfth Finance Commission II. Eight Finance Commission III. Second Finance Commission IV. Sixth Finance Commission","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":114,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The chairmen map as follows: K. Santhanam—Second Finance Commission (III); Brahmanand Reddy—Sixth (IV); Y. B. Chavan—Eighth (II); C. Rangarajan—Twelfth (I). Therefore A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I.","options":["Incorrect: the commission numbers for Santhanam, Reddy and Chavan are mismatched.","Incorrect: Santhanam and Chavan are mismatched.","Incorrect: Santhanam and Rangarajan are mismatched.","Correct: A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I."],"sources":[{"label":"Finance Commission chairperson chronology","url":"https://www.iassite.com/finance-commission-of-india-upsc/"},{"label":"Finance Commission chairpersons and terms","url":"https://shuunnya.org/learn/shuunnya-notes/indian-polity-notes/45-finance-commission/02-advisory-role"}]}},{"id":"J26-115","question":"Which of the following is not a characteristics of Public Policy?","options":["Purposive course of action","Formal authorization","Not express the will of state","Declaration of common goals."],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":115,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Public policy is a purposive course of action, formally authorized, and expresses public goals or the will of the state. The statement that it does not express the will of the state is incorrect.","options":["Correct: public policy is purposive.","Correct: public policy is generally formally authorized by public authorities.","Incorrect: public policy expresses the will/authoritative choices of the state; this is the false characteristic.","Correct: public policy commonly declares or pursues common goals."],"sources":[{"label":"Dye — Understanding Public Policy","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Thomas+Dye+Understanding+Public+Policy+definition"}]}},{"id":"J26-116","question":"Who among the following thinkers describe job enrichment as ‘the art of reshaping job.'?","options":["Victor Vroom","Frederick Herzberg","Robert Golembiewski","Ferrel Heady"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":116,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The job-enrichment concept is associated with Frederick Herzberg's two-factor theory and vertical loading of jobs. The phrase is attributed in this question's literature to Herzberg.","options":["Incorrect: Victor Vroom is associated with expectancy theory.","Correct: Frederick Herzberg is associated with job enrichment.","Incorrect: Robert Golembiewski is associated with organization development, not the foundational job-enrichment formulation.","Incorrect: Ferrel Heady is a comparative public administration scholar."],"sources":[{"label":"Herzberg — Work and the Nature of Man","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Frederick+Herzberg+job+enrichment+reshaping+job"}]}},{"id":"J26-117","question":"Which of the following statements are correct about Peter Drucker? A. The concept of Management by Objectives was not conceptualized by him. B. He introduced the concept of knowledge workers. C. He insists that the knowledge workers should learn to manage themselves. D. He described management as liberal art.","options":["A, B and C Only","B, C and D Only","A and B Only","C and D Only"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":117,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Drucker is associated with Management by Objectives, the concept of knowledge workers, and the idea that knowledge workers must learn to manage themselves. He also described management as a liberal art. Thus B, C and D are correct; A is false.","options":["Incorrect: Drucker is widely credited with developing/popularizing Management by Objectives.","Correct: Drucker introduced and popularized the term knowledge worker.","Correct: Drucker argued knowledge workers must manage themselves.","Correct: Drucker described management as a liberal art."],"sources":[{"label":"Harvard Business Review — Managing Oneself","url":"https://hbr.org/2005/01/managing-oneself"},{"label":"Peter Drucker — The Practice of Management","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Peter+Drucker+The+Practice+of+Management"}]}},{"id":"J26-118","question":"The Sala model is related to:","options":["Ecology in Prismatic societies","Economy in Prismatic societies","Culture in Prismatic societies","Administration in Prismatic societies"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":118,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"In Fred W. Riggs' prismatic-sala model, the sala is the administrative subsystem of the prismatic society.","options":["Incorrect: ecology is the broader framework, not the specific sala subsystem.","Incorrect: economy is not what the sala denotes.","Incorrect: culture is part of the prismatic setting, not the sala itself.","Correct: sala refers to the administrative subsystem in the prismatic model."],"sources":[{"label":"Riggs — Administration in Developing Countries","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Riggs+Administration+in+Developing+Countries+sala"}]}},{"id":"J26-119","question":"Arrange the following steps of research process: A. Hypothesis B. Literature Review C. Research Problem D. Report Writing E. Data Collection and Analysis","options":["A, B, C, D and E","B, C, A, D and E","C, B, A, E and D","D, A, B, C and E"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":119,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The logical sequence is: identify the research problem (C), review literature (B), formulate the hypothesis (A), collect and analyse data (E), and write the report (D). This is option 3.","options":["Incorrect: places hypothesis and report writing before the research problem and data work.","Incorrect: begins with literature review before defining the research problem and places report writing too early.","Correct: C, B, A, E and D.","Incorrect: starts with report writing and places the research problem too late."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact UGC NET research-process question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/arrange-the-following-steps-of-research-processa--6a8414b16cac6124707965c9/amp"},{"label":"Leeds Beckett University — stages in the research process","url":"https://libguides.leedsbeckett.ac.uk/skills-for-learning/research-skills"}]}},{"id":"J26-120","question":"Arrange the following in chronological order: A. National Commission on Urbanization. (CM Correa) B. Taxation Enquiry Commission (John Mathai) C. Local Finance Enquiry Committee (P.K. Wattal) D. Rural- Urban Relationship Committee (A..P. Jain) E. Committee on Budgetary Reform in Municipal Administration (Girijapati Mukhari)","options":["A, B, C, D, E","B, A, C, E, D","C, B, D, E, A","E, A, C, D, B"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":120,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The supported chronology is Local Finance Enquiry Committee (1949–51), Taxation Enquiry Commission (1953–54), Rural-Urban Relationship Committee (1963–66), Committee on Budgetary Reform in Municipal Administration (1974), and National Commission on Urbanisation (1985–88): C, B, D, E, A.","options":["Incorrect: places the 1980s National Commission first and reverses earlier committees.","Incorrect: places the Taxation Enquiry Commission before the earlier Local Finance Enquiry Committee and misorders the later bodies.","Correct: C, B, D, E, A follows the establishment chronology.","Incorrect: the 1974 committee and 1985–88 commission are not first."],"sources":[{"label":"Urban local government committees — chronology","url":"https://www.google.com/search?q=Local+Finance+Enquiry+Committee+Wattal+Taxation+Enquiry+Commission+John+Mathai+Rural+Urban+Relationship+Committee+chronology"},{"label":"National Commission on Urbanisation — report","url":"https://www.google.com/search?q=National+Commission+on+Urbanisation+1985+1988+CM+Correa"}]}},{"id":"J26-121","question":"Chronologically arrange the Second Administratives Reforms Commission Reports as per their report number A. Ethics in Governance B. Combating Terrorism: Protecting by Righteousness C. Organisational Structure of Government of India. D. Unlocking Human Capital : Entitlements and Governance E. Local Governance : An Inspiring Journey into the Future","options":["D, A, E, B, C","D, C, E, A, B","A, B, D, E, C","C, D, B, E, A"],"answer":1,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":121,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The report numbers are: Unlocking Human Capital—2nd (D); Ethics in Governance—4th (A); Local Governance—6th (E); Combating Terrorism—8th (B); Organisational Structure of Government of India—13th (C). The sequence is D, A, E, B, C.","options":["Correct: D, A, E, B, C follows report numbers 2, 4, 6, 8 and 13.","Incorrect: places the 13th report second and the 4th report fourth.","Incorrect: begins with the 4th and 8th reports instead of the 2nd.","Incorrect: places the 13th report first."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact Second ARC chronology question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/chronologically-arrange-the-second-administratives--6a8414ad20eeb7cce9ba9440"},{"label":"Second ARC report list and dates","url":"https://www.pani.org.in/2017/09/2nd-administrative-reforms-commission.html"}]}},{"id":"J26-122","question":"Which of the following is correct?","options":["Regulatory policies concerned with the distribution of new resources.","Redistributive policies are concerned with changing the distribution of existing resources.","Distributive policies are concerned with regulation and control of activities.","Constituent policy concerned with removing institution."],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":122,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Redistributive policies deliberately change the distribution of existing resources, wealth or benefits among groups. The other options confuse regulatory, distributive and constituent policy types.","options":["Incorrect: regulatory policies set rules and constraints, not distribution of new resources.","Correct: redistributive policies change the distribution of existing resources.","Incorrect: distributive policies allocate benefits/resources rather than regulate activities.","Incorrect: constituent policies establish or reorganize institutions and structures, not remove institutions as a defining feature."],"sources":[{"label":"Lowi — policy typologies","url":"https://www.google.com/search?q=Lowi+redistributive+policies+distribution+existing+resources+distributive+regulatory+constituent"}]}},{"id":"J26-123","question":"Match the LIST-I with LIST-II LIST-I (Thinker) A. Mary Parker Follett B. Abraham Maslow C. Chris Argyris D. Peter Drucker LIST-II (Inferential work) I. Personality and Organization II. The Practice of Management III. Motivation and Personality IV. Dynamic Administration","options":["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II"],"answer":4,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":123,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Follett's collected work is Dynamic Administration; Maslow wrote Motivation and Personality; Argyris wrote Personality and Organization; Drucker wrote The Practice of Management. The matching is A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II.","options":["Incorrect: assigns the works to the wrong authors.","Incorrect: Follett and Drucker are mismatched.","Incorrect: Maslow and Follett are mismatched.","Correct: A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II."],"sources":[{"label":"Follett — Dynamic Administration","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Mary+Parker+Follett+Dynamic+Administration"},{"label":"Maslow — Motivation and Personality","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Abraham+Maslow+Motivation+and+Personality"},{"label":"Argyris — Personality and Organization","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Chris+Argyris+Personality+and+Organization"},{"label":"Drucker — The Practice of Management","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Peter+Drucker+The+Practice+of+Management"}]}},{"id":"J26-124","question":"According to Mary Parker Follett, which among the following is not a way of resolving a conflict in an organization?","options":["Domination","Manipulation","Compromise","Integration"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":124,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Follett identified domination, compromise and integration as ways of dealing with conflict. Manipulation is not one of her three classic methods.","options":["Incorrect: domination is one of Follett's three methods.","Correct: manipulation is not one of the three methods.","Incorrect: compromise is one of the three methods.","Incorrect: integration is Follett's preferred constructive method."],"sources":[{"label":"Follett — Constructive Conflict / Dynamic Administration","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Mary+Parker+Follett+domination+compromise+integration"}]}},{"id":"J26-125","question":"Who presented among the following the \"Resolution on Industrial policy\" before the Constituent Assembly in April 1948?","options":["B.R. Ambedkar","Subhash Chandra Bose","Jawaharlal Nehru","Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":125,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The Industrial Policy Resolution was presented in the Constituent Assembly (Legislative) by Jawaharlal Nehru on 6–7 April 1948. The Nehru Archive preserves the speech and identifies it as Nehru's motion on the Government's industrial policy.","options":["Incorrect: Ambedkar did not present this resolution.","Incorrect: Subhash Chandra Bose had died in 1945 and could not have presented it in 1948.","Correct: Jawaharlal Nehru presented the industrial-policy motion in April 1948.","Incorrect: Patel was not the presenter identified in the Assembly record."],"sources":[{"label":"The Nehru Archive — Industrial Policy, 7 April 1948; Constituent Assembly speech","url":"https://nehruarchive.in/documents/industrial-policy-7-april-1948-d2ok4"}]}},{"id":"J26-126","question":"The Comptroller and Auditor General of India presents its report to the parliament through which committee:","options":["Estimates Committee","Public Accounts Committee","Committee on Public Undertakings","Departmental Standing Committee"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":126,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The Comptroller and Auditor General's reports relating to Union accounts are examined by the Public Accounts Committee, which scrutinizes government expenditure and audit findings.","options":["Incorrect: Estimates Committee examines estimates and economy, not CAG audit reports in the PAC role.","Correct: Public Accounts Committee examines CAG reports.","Incorrect: Committee on Public Undertakings focuses on public sector undertakings.","Incorrect: departmental standing committees do not replace PAC's constitutional-budgetary audit role."],"sources":[{"label":"Lok Sabha — Public Accounts Committee","url":"https://sansad.in/ls/committees"}]}},{"id":"J26-127","question":"Which of the following is correct regarding District Disaster Management Authorities?","options":["District Disaster Management Authorities formulate policy for national level.","District Disaster Management Authorities work under Zila Pramukh (Head of Zila Parishad).","District Collector / Deputy Commissioner contacts with the Nation Disaster Response Force (NDRF) for disaster response.","District Disaster Management Authority does not associate Civil Society in disaster relief operations."],"answer":null,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":127,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Flagged — conflicting source explanation","basis":"The exact question-specific Testbook page labels option 3 as the answer in its heading, but its detailed explanation then states option 2. The Disaster Management Act, 2005, section 25, says the Collector/District Magistrate/Deputy Commissioner chairs the DDMA and the elected local-authority representative is co-chair; where a Zila Parishad exists, its chairperson is co-chair. It does not say the DDMA works under a Zila Pramukh. The Act also allows civil-society representatives among appointed members (as amended). Since the source solution contradicts itself and option 3's operational wording is not a statutory rule, the item is flagged pending a stronger primary source for the NDRF coordination claim.","options":["Incorrect: national-level disaster policy is not formulated by the district authority.","Incorrect: the Act does not place the DDMA under the Zila Pramukh; the Collector chairs it and the Zila Parishad chairperson may be co-chair.","Not established by the Act's constitution provision; this is the answer label shown by one part of the question-specific source, but its explanation conflicts.","Incorrect: the Act expressly allows civil-society organisation representatives among appointed members."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact DDMA question; answer heading and explanation conflict","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/which-of-the-following-is-correct-regarding-distri--6a8414a295023b632305139b/amp"},{"label":"India Code — Disaster Management Act, 2005, section 25","url":"https://www.indiacode.nic.in/bitstream/123456789/19269/1/dm_act_2005_english-1.pdf"}]}},{"id":"J26-128","question":"According to Edward S. Quade, the decision making process involves five key stages A. Search B. Formulation C. Modeling D. Forecasting E. Evaluation","options":["A, B, C, D and E","B, A, D, C and E","C, A, D, B and E","D, E, A, B and C"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":128,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The five stages in Quade's sequence are Formulation (B), Search (A), Forecasting (D), Modelling (C), and Evaluation (E). The exact question-specific solution identifies B, A, D, C, E, which is option 2.","options":["Incorrect: puts the stages in the order listed rather than Quade's sequence.","Correct: B, A, D, C and E.","Incorrect: begins with modelling and does not follow the problem-analysis sequence.","Incorrect: begins with forecasting and evaluation before formulation and search."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact Edward S. Quade question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/according-to-edward-s-quade-the-decision-making--6a841495b97d1184d20d54bd"}]}},{"id":"J26-129","question":"Which of the following is not correct regarding disaster.?","options":["Disaster is an event which falls under the coping capacity of the affected community.","Natural disasters take place at a place which is vulnerable.","Careful pre-disaster planning and preparedness can reduce the adverse effect of hazards and economic loss.","Man is also responsible for disasters in society but man-made disasters can be managed and controlled."],"answer":1,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":129,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"A disaster is a serious disruption whose impacts may exceed the affected community's ability to cope using its own resources. Option A reverses this definition by saying a disaster falls within the community's coping capacity. The other statements describe vulnerability, preparedness and human-caused hazards in broadly valid terms.","options":["Correct: this is not correct; the definition is that impacts may exceed the community's coping capacity.","Incorrect: disaster risk is shaped by hazard and vulnerability/exposure at a place.","Incorrect: preparedness and pre-disaster planning can reduce impacts and losses.","Incorrect: human activities can cause disasters, and many human-made risks can be mitigated or controlled."],"sources":[{"label":"UN Office for Disaster Risk Reduction — definition of disaster","url":"https://www.undrr.org/terminology/disaster"},{"label":"India — National Disaster Management Plan","url":"https://origin1504-mha.nic.in/sites/default/files/2022-08/National%2520Disaster%2520Management%2520Plan%2520May%25202016%5B1%5D.pdf"}]}},{"id":"J26-130","question":"Which of the following article stipulates that the Governor shall be appointed by the President of India by warrant under his hand and seal","options":["153","154","155","156"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":130,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Article 155 of the Constitution provides that the Governor of a State shall be appointed by the President by warrant under his hand and seal.","options":["Incorrect: Article 153 provides for Governors of States.","Incorrect: Article 154 vests the executive power of the State in the Governor.","Correct: Article 155 specifies appointment by the President by warrant under his hand and seal.","Incorrect: Article 156 deals with the term of office of the Governor."],"sources":[{"label":"Constitution of India — Article 155","url":"https://legislative.gov.in/constitution-of-india/"}]}},{"id":"J26-131","question":"Among the following authors, who said ‘Administration cannot be divorced from its connections with the other branches of public law without being distorted and robbed of its true significance.'","options":["Luther Gulick","Frank J. Goodnow","Woodrow Wilson","W.F. Willoughby"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":131,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The quotation is attributed to Frank J. Goodnow. It emphasizes that administration must be understood in connection with public law and the legal structure of the state.","options":["Incorrect: this quotation is not attributed to Gulick.","Correct: Frank J. Goodnow is identified with the quotation.","Incorrect: Wilson is associated with the 1887 essay The Study of Administration, not this wording.","Incorrect: Willoughby wrote on public administration and public law, but the quoted line is attributed to Goodnow."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact quotation and attribution","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/among-the-following-authors-who-said-administrat--6a8414a36e24783a19cfba54/amp"}]}},{"id":"J26-132","question":"Match the LIST-I with LIST-II LIST-I A. Atal Vayo Abhyuday Yojna B. One Stop Centre C. e-Sanjeevani D. Tele MANAS LIST-II I. A scheme to improve the quality of life of senior citizens II. National Tele Medicine Service III. Sakhi Centres provide integrated service to women affected by violence IV. Offering immediate mental health support.","options":["A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV"],"answer":1,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":132,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Atal Vayo Abhyuday Yojana concerns senior citizens; One Stop Centres (Sakhi) support women affected by violence; e-Sanjeevani is the National Telemedicine Service; Tele-MANAS provides tele-mental-health support. The mapping is A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV.","options":["Correct: all four schemes/services are correctly matched.","Incorrect: e-Sanjeevani and Tele-MANAS are reversed.","Incorrect: the scheme and service pairings are mismatched.","Incorrect: Atal Vayo Abhyuday Yojana is not a telemedicine service."],"sources":[{"label":"Ministry of Social Justice — AVYAY","url":"https://socialjustice.gov.in/"},{"label":"Ministry of Women and Child Development — One Stop Centre","url":"https://wcd.gov.in/"},{"label":"Ministry of Health — e-Sanjeevani","url":"https://esanjeevani.mohfw.gov.in/"},{"label":"Ministry of Health — Tele-MANAS","url":"https://telemanas.mohfw.gov.in/"}]}},{"id":"J26-133","question":"Who of the following defined, \"Comparative Public Administration is the comparative study of institutions, processes and behaviors in many contexts. In a comparative analysis, context generally refers to external influences that affect management, such as societal values, norms, religion, political culture and economy.\"","options":["Karl Deutsch","Sigelman and Gadbois","Jamil Jreisat","Nicholas Henry"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":133,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The definition is attributed to Jamil Jreisat. It treats comparative public administration as the comparison of institutions, processes and behaviours across contexts, with context including social values, norms, religion, political culture and the economy.","options":["Incorrect: Karl Deutsch is not the source of this specific definition.","Incorrect: the wording is not attributed to Sigelman and Gadbois.","Correct: Jamil Jreisat is credited with this definition.","Incorrect: Nicholas Henry is not the author of this specific formulation."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact UGC NET definition question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/who-of-the-following-defined-comparative-pu--6a8414a9c03132905a3a1923"}]}},{"id":"J26-134","question":"Which of the following is the demerit of the interviews method used to collect primary data?","options":["Observation method can as well be applied to recording verbal answers to various questions.","This method is relatively more time-consuming, specially when the sample is large and recalls upon the respondents are necessary.","Personal information can as well be obtained easily under this method","More information and that too in greater depth can be obtained"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":134,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The major disadvantage stated is that interviews are time-consuming, particularly with large samples and where respondents must be recalled. Options C and D describe advantages of interviews; A is not the stated disadvantage.","options":["Incorrect: this is not the main disadvantage identified in the item.","Correct: interviews can be time-consuming, especially for large samples and repeated contact.","Incorrect: obtaining personal information can be an advantage of interviews.","Incorrect: obtaining deeper information is an advantage, not a disadvantage."],"sources":[{"label":"Kothari — Research Methodology","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=C+R+Kothari+Research+Methodology+interview+time+consuming"}]}},{"id":"J26-135","question":"Match the LIST-I with LIST-II LIST-I (Books) A. Administrative Theories and Politics B. Policy and Administration C. New Public Administration D. The Intellectual Crisis in American Public Administration LIST-II (Authors) I. H. George Frederickson II. Peter Self III. Frank J. Goodnow IV. Vincent Ostrom","options":["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":135,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The matches are: Administrative Theories and Politics—Peter Self (II); Policy and Administration—Frank J. Goodnow (III); New Public Administration—H. George Frederickson (I); The Intellectual Crisis in American Public Administration—Vincent Ostrom (IV). Therefore A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV.","options":["Incorrect: misattributes Administrative Theories and Politics and Policy and Administration.","Correct: A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV.","Incorrect: assigns the books to the wrong authors.","Incorrect: assigns Administrative Theories and Politics to Ostrom and The Intellectual Crisis to Goodnow."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact UGC NET book/author question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/match-the-list-i-with-list-i-bo--6a8414a215917f70d21a9a65"},{"label":"University of Hyderabad library catalogue — Peter Self, Administrative Theories and Politics","url":"https://igmlnet.uohyd.ac.in/chamo/lib/item?copies-sort=2+asc&id=chamo%3A108131&theme=hcu"}]}},{"id":"J26-136","question":"The \"Target Base Budgeting' is also known as:","options":["Top-Down Budgeting","Zero-Base Budgeting","Budgeting-by-objectives","Performance-based Budgeting"],"answer":1,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":136,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Target Base Budgeting is identified in the question-specific reference as Top-Down Budgeting: senior management sets overall spending targets or ceilings before lower-level units prepare detailed allocations.","options":["Correct: Target Base Budgeting is also called Top-Down Budgeting in the cited examination reference.","Incorrect: Zero-Base Budgeting requires justification of expenditure from a zero base.","Incorrect: Budgeting-by-objectives links resources to objectives but is not the synonym used here.","Incorrect: performance-based budgeting links allocations to outputs or performance measures."],"sources":[{"label":"Testbook — exact Target Base Budgeting question","url":"https://testbook.com/question-answer/the-target-base-budgeting-is-also-known-as--6a8414a26d3976b0d1abe61f/amp"}]}},{"id":"J26-137","question":"Which of the following statements is correct about Digital-Era Governance? A. Focuses not to provide need-oriented services to the citizens. B. It uses information and communication technologies. C. Focuses on providing holistic services to citizens. D. It uses social media for public service delivery.","options":["A, B & D Only","B, C & D Only","C & D Only","A, B, C & D Only"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":137,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Digital-Era Governance emphasizes reintegration, needs-based/holistic services and the use of digital technologies. Statement A is false because it says the approach does not focus on need-oriented services; B, C and D are treated as correct in the question's framing.","options":["Incorrect: A contradicts the citizen-oriented, needs-based service focus.","Correct: digital-era governance uses ICT.","Correct: reintegration and holistic services are central themes.","Correct in the broad contemporary e-government sense: digital and social platforms can support public service delivery."],"sources":[{"label":"Dunleavy et al. — Digital-Era Governance","url":"https://www.google.com/search?q=Dunleavy+Digital-Era+Governance+reintegration+holistic+services+ICT"},{"label":"OECD — Digital Government","url":"https://www.oecd.org/gov/digital-government/"}]}},{"id":"J26-138","question":"Arrange the ingredient of Shannon and Weaver and Schramm model of communication in order A. Source and encoding B. Channel C. Decoding and receiver D. Feedback E. Message","options":["A, B, C, D, E","A, E, B, C, D","C, B, A, D, E","D, C, B, A, E"],"answer":2,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":138,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"The sequence is source/encoder, message, channel, receiver/decoder, followed by feedback in the interactive Schramm extension: A, E, B, C, D. Feedback is not part of Shannon and Weaver's original one-way mathematical model, so the question combines two related models.","options":["Incorrect: puts channel before the message and feedback before the message.","Correct: source/encoding → message → channel → decoding/receiver → feedback.","Incorrect: starts with receiver/decoding and reverses the communication flow.","Incorrect: starts with feedback and reverses the sender-to-receiver sequence."],"sources":[{"label":"Shannon and Weaver — The Mathematical Theory of Communication","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Shannon+Weaver+Mathematical+Theory+of+Communication"},{"label":"Schramm — The Process and Effects of Mass Communication","url":"https://books.google.com/books?q=Wilbur+Schramm+The+Process+and+Effects+of+Mass+Communication"}]}},{"id":"J26-139","question":"Which of the following statements are correct about Civil Services in India. A. In independent India, the Indian Administrative Service (IAS) succeeded the Indian Civil Service B. Jawahar Lal Nehru was critic of the Indian Civil Service for its role in sustaining the imperial rule. C. The constitution under Article 312 contains the provision for setting up of All India Services. D. All India Services serve the Union Government only. E. The Central Services belong to Central Government and serve different departments of the Central Government.","options":["A, B, D Only","A, B, C, D Only","A, B, C, E Only","A, C, D, E Only"],"answer":3,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":139,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"A is true: IAS succeeded the ICS after independence. B is consistent with Nehru's criticism of the colonial ICS. C is true: Article 312 provides for All India Services. D is false because All India Services serve both the Union and State governments. E is true for Central Services. Thus A, B, C and E only.","options":["Incorrect: D is false; All India Services serve both Union and States.","Incorrect: includes D, which is false, and omits E.","Correct: A, B, C and E only.","Incorrect: includes D and omits B."],"sources":[{"label":"Constitution of India — Article 312","url":"https://legislative.gov.in/constitution-of-india/"},{"label":"DoPT — All India Services","url":"https://dopt.gov.in/"}]}},{"id":"J26-140","question":"Which of the following is correct about the Comptroller and Auditor-General of India:-","options":["The Comptroller and Auditor-General of India is appointed by the President under his hand and seal.","Retirement age of Comptroller and Auditor-General of India is fixed at 66 years.","Comptroller and Auditor-General of India gets the same salary as a Chief Justice of India.","Comptroller and Auditor-General of India is responsible for maintenance of accounts of the Union Government."],"answer":1,"set":"June 2026","sourceQuestion":140,"unit":null,"analysis":{"status":"Independently verified","basis":"Article 148 provides for the CAG's appointment by the President by warrant under his hand and seal. The CAG's salary is equal to that of a Judge of the Supreme Court, not the Chief Justice of India: the CJI has a distinct higher salary. The CAG holds office for six years or until age 65, whichever is earlier. Union government accounts are maintained by the Controller General of Accounts following departmentalisation. Thus A is the sole correct statement.","options":["Correct: Article 148(1) provides for appointment by the President by warrant under his hand and seal.","Incorrect: the CAG vacates office at age 65 or after six years, whichever is earlier.","Incorrect: the statutory salary is equal to that of a Judge of the Supreme Court; the Chief Justice of India has a separate higher salary.","Incorrect: Union government accounts are maintained by the Controller General of Accounts, not the CAG."],"sources":[{"label":"Constitution of India — Article 148","url":"https://legislative.gov.in/constitution-of-india/"},{"label":"CAG — Duties, Powers and Conditions of Service Act, sections 3 and 4","url":"https://www.cag.gov.in/en/pages/single/3"},{"label":"Controller General of Accounts — Departmentalisation of Accounts","url":"https://cga.nic.in/"}]}}];
